|------|--------------|
| **Goal‑stop (`h > 1.03`) → action = 1** | Once the free end is above the required height we should stop injecting energy, otherwise we will swing back down and incur many extra -1 steps. |
| **Pump when the first link is near the vertical down position and moving down (`θ₁≈0, w₁>0`) → action = 2** | The reaction torque on joint 1 pushes it forward, increasing the total mechanical energy – exactly the “swing‑up” trick. |
| **Pump when the first link is near the vertical up position and moving up (`θ₁≈π, w₁<0`) → action = 0** | The same reaction now adds energy in the upward direction (the second joint is torqued opposite to the motion of the first link). |
| **Middle‑of‑swing use of w₂ sign** | When neither joint is close to the vertical, the easiest way to keep the swing alive is to apply torque in the direction of the current angular velocity of the second joint (classic “energy‑pump”). |
| **Energy‑dump phase (`h` just above goal) → torque opposite to w₂** | Prevents the system from overshooting back below the goal; it damps the swing quickly so the episode ends with the minimum number of -1 steps. |

